The screen shows LeetCode 4, “Median of Two Sorted Arrays,” requiring JavaScript solve(nums1, nums2) with no input/output wrapper. Binary-search a partition in the shorter array until every value on the left is no greater than every value on the right; then return the middle value or the average of the two middle values. This takes O(log(min(m, n) + 1)) time and O(1) extra space, including when one array is empty.

```javascript
function solve(nums1, nums2) {
  let a = nums1;
  let b = nums2;
  if (a.length > b.length) [a, b] = [b, a];

  const m = a.length;
  const n = b.length;
  const half = Math.floor((m + n + 1) / 2);
  let low = 0;
  let high = m;

  while (low <= high) {
    const i = low + Math.floor((high - low) / 2);
    const j = half - i;

    const aLeft = i === 0 ? -Infinity : a[i - 1];
    const aRight = i === m ? Infinity : a[i];
    const bLeft = j === 0 ? -Infinity : b[j - 1];
    const bRight = j === n ? Infinity : b[j];

    if (aLeft <= bRight && bLeft <= aRight) {
      const leftMax = Math.max(aLeft, bLeft);
      if ((m + n) % 2 === 1) return leftMax;
      return (leftMax + Math.min(aRight, bRight)) / 2;
    }

    if (aLeft > bRight) high = i - 1;
    else low = i + 1;
  }

  throw new Error('Inputs must be sorted ascending.');
}
```

Submit this function directly. Inputs must be ascending numeric arrays with a positive combined length: solve([1, 3], [2]) returns 2; solve([1, 2], [3, 4]) returns 2.5; solve([], [1]) returns 1.

