The screen shows LeetCode 115, Distinct Subsequences: count how many ways deleting characters from s produces t. Use dynamic programming, updating backward so each source character is used at most once.

```javascript
function solve(s, t) {
  if (t.length > s.length) return 0;

  const dp = new Array(t.length + 1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  const LIMIT = 2147483647;

  for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {
    for (let j = Math.min(t.length, i + 1); j >= 1; j--) {
      if (s[i] === t[j - 1]) {
        dp[j] = Math.min(LIMIT, dp[j] + dp[j - 1]);
      }
    }
  }

  return dp[t.length];
}
```

dp[j] counts ways to form the first j characters of t from the processed source characters. Counts are capped to avoid precision loss; since updates only add nonnegative counts and the answer fits a signed 32-bit integer, this preserves the result. Call solve(s, t) directly; no input/output wrapper is needed.

Time: O(s.length × t.length). Space: O(t.length).

