The screen shows Two Sum: return two distinct indices whose values add to target. Use a map of previously seen values; checking before insertion prevents reusing the same index.

```javascript
function solve(nums, target) {
  const seen = new Map();
  for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
    const complement = target - nums[i];
    if (seen.has(complement)) {
      return [seen.get(complement), i];
    }
    seen.set(nums[i], i);
  }
  return [];
}
```

Call solve(nums, target) directly; no input/output wrapper is needed. Expected time: O(n). Space: O(n).

